course: euphemisms
lesson: 23
title: Soft words for hard things: business and advertising and politics and government spin
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Companies hide extra costs, broken products and bad results behind soft words; politicians hide taxes and untruths the same way.
explain: A "convenience fee" sounds like a favour, but it is simply an extra charge for paying the way the shop chose for you.
explain: Programmers joke that a bug is an "undocumented feature", and a firm that lost a fortune may call it "a learning experience".
explain: In politics, someone caught saying something false may say they "misspoke", and a new tax can be called "revenue enhancement".
explain: Ask what the plain word would be: a fee, a bug, a loss, a tax, a lie.
fact: "Convenience fee" means an extra charge for paying online or by card (US).
fact: "Service charge" means an extra fee added to the bill (UK).
fact: "New look, same great taste" can mean a smaller package for the same price.
fact: "Value" on a shop's own brand can mean cheap and of low quality.
fact: "Known issue" means a bug (a mistake in a computer program) that has not been fixed.
fact: "Undocumented feature" is a joking name for a software bug.
fact: "Sunsetting" means ending or discontinuing a product: the firm stops making or running it.
fact: "Legacy system" means old, outdated software.
fact: "Creative accounting" means dishonest or misleading bookkeeping (the written record of a firm's money).
fact: "A learning experience" often means an expensive failure.
fact: "Pivot" often means a change of plan because the first plan failed.
fact: "Headwinds" in business means problems that hurt sales.
fact: "Challenging trading conditions" means business is bad and losing money (UK).
fact: "Dynamic pricing" means raising prices when demand is high, that is, when lots of people want to buy.
fact: "Revenue enhancement" means a tax increase (US).
fact: "User fee" means a tax or charge for using a public service (US).
fact: "Alternative facts" means falsehoods, things that are not true.
fact: "Misspoke" means said something false.
fact: "Terminological inexactitude" is a joking name for a lie (UK).
fact: "Economical with the truth" means lying or leaving out important facts (UK).
exercise: What is a "convenience fee" on a ticket website? | An extra charge for paying online or by card | A discount for buying your tickets early in the year | A free gift for loyal buyers | A tip for the ticket seller
exercise: What is a "service charge" on a UK restaurant bill? | An extra fee added to the bill | The price of the main course | A fine for bad behaviour | A refund for slow service
exercise: What can "new look, same great taste" on a packet really mean? | A smaller package for the same price | A bigger package at no extra cost | A brand new recipe with fresh ingredients | A cheaper price for the same size
exercise: What does a "value" range in a supermarket often mean? | Cheap and of low quality | Very valuable and rare | The best quality in the shop | Healthy and organic
exercise: What does a software company mean by a "known issue"? | A bug that has not been fixed | A famous problem in maths | A question everyone knows | A news story about the firm
exercise: What is an "undocumented feature" when a programmer says it as a joke? | A software bug | A secret bonus level | A tool with no manual yet | A new feature for experts
exercise: What does it mean when a company is "sunsetting" a product? | Ending or discontinuing it | Making it work at night | Painting it orange, like a sunset sky | Selling it only in summer
exercise: What is a "legacy system"? | Old, outdated software | Software left in a will | Software for history lessons | The newest computer system
exercise: What is "creative accounting"? | Dishonest or misleading bookkeeping | Drawing pictures of money | Counting with colourful pens and glitter | Teaching maths through art
exercise: What does a business usually mean when it calls a disaster "a learning experience"? | An expensive failure | A school trip for the staff | A training course | A big success
exercise: What does a start-up (a new small company) often mean when it says it will "pivot"? | Change plans because the first plan failed | Turn all the office furniture round to face the window | Grow very fast | Move to a new country for fun
exercise: What do business reports mean by "headwinds"? | Problems that hurt sales | Strong winds at the airport | Air conditioning in the office | Good news coming soon
exercise: What does "challenging trading conditions" mean in a UK company report? | Business is bad and losing money | The shop is running a quiz | Prices are fixed by law | Business is too busy to cope
exercise: What is "dynamic pricing"? | Raising prices when demand is high | Prices that are printed in bright colours | Lowering prices every hour | Prices fixed for the whole year
exercise: What does a US politician mean by "revenue enhancement"? | A tax increase | A pay rise for nurses | A better tax website | A cut in taxes
exercise: What is a "user fee" for a public park? | A tax or charge for using it | A reward for visiting often | A tip for the park ranger | A free pass for children
exercise: What are "alternative facts"? | Falsehoods | Facts from a second source | Surprising true facts | Facts that change each year
exercise: What does it mean when a politician says he "misspoke"? | He said something false | He spoke too quietly | He used the wrong language | He spoke for too long
exercise: What is a "terminological inexactitude"? | A lie | A spelling mistake | A difficult word | A long speech
exercise: What does it mean if someone was "economical with the truth"? | He lied or left out facts | He told the truth cheaply | He saved money by being honest | He spoke very briefly
test: "ugh, the concert ticket was 40 but they added a convenience fee of 6" — how much did they pay in all? | 46 | 40 | 34 | 6
test: News: "The minister said yesterday's figures were wrong and that he had misspoken." What does this admit? | That what he said was false | That his microphone broke | That he was misheard | That he spoke in French
test: A cereal box says "New look, same great taste!" and it costs the same. What should a shopper check? | Whether the box has less in it | Whether the colour is nice | Whether the taste changed | Whether there is a toy
test: "Lol it's not a bug, it's an undocumented feature" — what is the speaker really saying? | It is a bug, said as a joke | It is a secret extra | It is a planned feature for later | It is a manual
test: The app maker writes: "We are sunsetting this app on 1 May." What will happen? | The app will stop being available | The app will get a dark mode | The app will only work at sunset | The app will become free
test: Taxi prices doubled during the storm. What is this called? | Dynamic pricing | A service charge | A user fee | A price cut
test: Report: "Our sales fell because of headwinds and challenging trading conditions." What does the firm admit? | Business went badly | The wind blew down the shop | Business went brilliantly | Staff traded jobs
test: The boss says: "Losing that million was a learning experience." What happened? | The firm lost a lot of money | The staff went on a course | The firm won a prize | The firm opened a school
test: A politician says: "This is not a tax rise, it is revenue enhancement." Is it a tax rise? | Yes, it is a tax rise with a softer name | No, it is a tax cut | No, it is a kind gift to all the people of the country | No, it is a new bank
test: Jake says: "Alternative facts are facts from a different book, so they're true too." Is he right? | No, the phrase is used for falsehoods | Yes, they are just as true | Yes, they are newer facts | No, they are facts about other planets
test: "the bank still runs on some legacy system from the 80s, total pain" — what does the bank use? | Old, outdated software | Software from a famous will | Brand new software | Paper only
test: An auditor (someone who checks a firm's money records) finds "creative accounting" in the books. What has she found? | Misleading or dishonest figures | Pretty drawings in the margins | A new art budget | Very accurate figures
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the expression "economical with the truth" as applied to a public official's statement. | The statement was untruthful or omitted material facts | The statement was brief but entirely accurate | The statement was delivered without remuneration | The statement reduced public expenditure
exam: It is given that a corporation has designated a defect in its software a "known issue". Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The defect is acknowledged but remains unrectified | The defect has been fully rectified | The defect has never been observed by any user | The defect is a deliberate enhancement
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice of "dynamic pricing"? | The elevation of prices at times of heightened demand | The publication of prices in vivid colours | The reduction of prices at fixed intervals | The maintenance of identical prices throughout the year
exam: Ascertain the plain meaning of "revenue enhancement" when employed by a legislator in respect of fiscal policy. | An increase in taxation | A reduction in taxation | An increase in public salaries | An improvement to the collection website
